<!--
  PLAYER play glyph - official Google Material.

  Source: Material Symbols Rounded `play_arrow`, FILL 0, wght 400, GRAD 0,
  opsz 24, from github.com/google/material-design-icons @ master,
  `symbols/web/play_arrow/materialsymbolsrounded/play_arrow_24px.svg`
  (Apache License 2.0). The pathData below is that file's, verbatim.

  **This supersedes the owner-final Figma pair** in
  tools/figma-export/player-icons/owner-final, by an explicit owner decision
  taken after the two were compared on device. Those files stay where they are as
  the record of what they replaced; nothing reads them any more.

  Why the app is better off with Google's: the custom pair had two problems that
  could only have been answered with per-glyph overrides, and this answers both
  without any.

   - **Optical centring.** A triangle's visual mass sits at its centroid, not at
     the middle of its bounding box, so a play glyph whose box is centred reads
     as sitting left. The custom glyph measured its mass 4.386dp left of the
     control centre. Material's carries Google's own correction - the box is
     +2.125dp right so the mass lands on centre, measured at -0.5dp - so no
     translation is applied here or anywhere else.
   - **Size against pause.** The custom play was 24% taller than the custom
     pause; Material's play is slightly smaller than its pause, which is the
     relationship that reads as equal weight. No scale is applied either.

  FILL 0, so it is hollow, like the stroked pair it replaces: the subpath after
  the outer contour is wound against it, and the default nonZero rule - which is
  both VectorDrawable's and SVG's, i.e. the source's own - leaves the middle
  open. Its wall measures 3.97dp against the superseded stroke's 4.00. No
  `fillType` is declared, because declaring one would be an edit.

  The source viewBox is "0 -960 960 960", the Material Symbols convention, whose
  origin is the baseline rather than the top-left. VectorDrawable has no negative
  viewport origin, so the path is carried into the 0..960 box by a group
  translate of +960 in Y. That is a change of coordinates, not of shape, and it
  is the only thing done to the file.

  Nominal 48dp - an official Material size, shared with ic_player_pause.xml so
  the pair cannot drift - drawn by `scaleType=center` inside the 80x80 control.
  The surface is NOT here: it is bg_player_play_pause, the button's own
  background, kept separate so PlayerControl can swap this glyph for the
  buffering indicator without the fill ever leaving the screen.

  The fill is the repo's #FFFFFF placeholder; the colour is `player_play_glyph`,
  applied as a tint from the layout, in Light and Dark alike.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="@dimen/player_play_glyph_size"
    android:height="@dimen/player_play_glyph_size"
    android:viewportWidth="960"
    android:viewportHeight="960">
    <group android:translateY="960">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFF"
